<<~ ahu #/about>>

! Vendor Declaration — the Claude Code Harness

The primary agent surface this operator runs, and a vendor the stack ''composes with at
arm's length'' rather than depends on. A vendor declaration names what an outside surface
offers, on what contract, at what stability — never what the house must become.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/evidence-grades>>

!! Evidence Grades — read before citing anything here

Three grades, and they do not carry equal weight:

* ''RAW'' — the literal doc markdown, pulled and read. Every doc page fetches as markdown by
appending `.md` to its URL (`code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks.md`). ''Use this grade for
anything that enters a build.''
* ''REPORTED'' — a summarizing fetch layer stood between the page and the reader. On one page
where both grades ran, the summarizer ''invented four field names'' that RAW disproved.
* ''DIGEST'' — a search-engine summary of a source that refused the fetch. Caps near 12/20
however confident the wording sounds.

''The collision discipline: before a claim here bears weight, pull the
page RAW and read the section.'' Three claims carried that collision on capture — the
`ConfigChange` contract, the `additionalContext` cap, and the resume semantics — and each
held.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-published-seat>>

!! The Published Seat

The vendor names its own harness a replaceable component. Its
managed-agents engineering writing decouples ''the brain (the model and its harness)'' from
''the hands (sandboxes and tools)'' and ''the session (the log of events)'' — each an
interface making few assumptions about the others, each replaceable independently, and the
architecture stated as unopinionated about which harness comes next.

''So an independent system needs no crack to fit through.'' The socket stands published.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-seams>>

!! The Seams, ranked by which way the dependency runs

| Rank | Seam | Why it holds | Grade |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | ''MCP server'' | the harness dials out to the daemon; versioned protocol, independent spec body | RAW |
| 2 | ''Portable skill format'' | an open standard with a reference validator, read by many harnesses | RAW |
| 3 | ''Headless subprocess'' | the vendor's own documented path for callers outside its two SDK languages | RAW |
| 4 | ''Hooks'' | deterministic where instructions stay advisory — and the fastest-moving surface here | RAW |

''Rank 1 carries the load.'' A daemon the harness connects to inverts
the dependency: the sovereign process stands first and answers, rather than living inside
something else's lifecycle.

!!! The portable skill format

Six frontmatter fields stand legal outside the vendor's own harness: `name`, `description`,
`license`, `compatibility`, `metadata`, `allowed-tools`. The harness accepts a much larger
superset, and any of it ''hard-errors'' on the portable path rather than warning.
''A projection emitted from meme canon therefore MUST hold to the six.''

!!! Hooks — the contracts that matter to this house

''`ConfigChange`'' fires on user, project, local, policy and skill
configuration edits, carrying `source` and `file_path` — the drift surface a posture reviewer
watches. RAW: a hook MAY block the change, and ''a blocked change surfaces no message to the
operator or to the agent — only a debug-log line''. ''So on this seam, acting runs invisible
and only observing speaks''
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/kairos#refusal">>).

''The frame ceiling.'' Hook output strings, `additionalContext`
included, cap at 10,000 characters; past it the text lands in a file and the exchange
receives a preview plus a path. ''One shared budget across every hook that speaks at a
wake.''

''Resume holds the frame honest.'' Mid-session hook context replays
from the transcript on resume — stale timestamps and all — while session-start hooks ''run
again'', carrying a source that names the resume. A wake frame promising what stands right
now keeps that promise only from the session-start seat.

''A hook renders no security boundary, by the vendor's own statement''
— a hook that times out yields no decision and the action proceeds. Permission rules, not
hooks, carry a hard allow or deny.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-laws-we-adopt>>

!! The Laws This House Adopts

Each stands substrate-neutral — true of any harness, ours included.

''Context runs finite, with diminishing returns.'' Recall degrades as
a window fills; the design objective reads as the smallest set of high-signal tokens that
carries the outcome. ''The transclusion substrate enacts this''
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/context-substrate">>).

''Just-in-time retrieval over pre-loading.'' Hold lightweight
identifiers; load content at runtime through them. ''A `lar:` URI stands as that
identifier.''

''Fewer, better tools.'' A crowded surface distracts an agent from
efficient strategies. ''Richness belongs in the addresses, never in the verb count.''

''Never let a federation surface enumerate itself into an exchange.''
Cheap composition at a protocol layer turns expensive at the attention layer — tool
definitions alone have overloaded windows before a first request. ''A mesh that lets peers
announce their surfaces inherits that bill.''

''The environment holds where supervision does not.'' Approval
prompts measure ~93% approved, and attention thins as approvals multiply; in a measured
injection trial the exfiltration completed 24 times in 25 retries, and only egress control
held. ''Supervise what an agent CAN do, never only what it does.''

''A house hydrating a large seed at every wake sits inside a tension
the vendor names and never solves'' — persistent-instruction surfaces grow, and a bloated one
gets ignored wholesale. ''Measure the seed rather than assume it.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/traps>>

!! Traps

''A headless run without the bare flag executes an untrusted
repository's hooks and connects its declared servers'' — no trust dialog, no per-server
prompt. Anything here that shells out to the harness carries that flag from its first line.

''The protocol's sampling primitive stands deprecated'' — the path
that let a server borrow the host's model withdraws, and the guidance names direct provider
integration instead. ''Nothing local-first depends on it.''

''One switch disables three seams'' — the hook kill-switch also takes
the custom status line and the file-suggestion command. A design spread across all three
loses all three together.

''A pattern bounds a string; only a runtime bounds authority.''
Wherever an allowed program can name another program, an allow-list has delegated its whole
authority to text the pattern never sees
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/kairos#check-set-one">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/stability>>

!! Stability

| Surface | Machine-readable schema | Read |
|---|---|---|
| the protocol | versioned revisions, independent body | ''safest'' |
| the portable skill format | published spec + reference validator | ''safe'' |
| hooks | none served | documented, weekly-moving — version-gate every use |
| settings · plugins · subagents · status line | none served | documented, proprietary |
| the streamed output shape | none served | feature-detect on the capability array the stream carries |

''No machine-checkable artifact stands behind hooks, settings, plugin
or marketplace manifests, or the streamed message union.'' Prose in docs, all of it. A gate
built against them tests behaviour, never a schema.

<<~/ahu>>

